<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Diauehi</h1><p class="st-meta">1120 BCE – 760 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A tribal union in the highlands of north-eastern Anatolia, known from Assyrian and Urartian war records and often linked with the later Georgian region of Tao.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Known almost entirely from the inscriptions of its enemies. The Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-i">Tiglath-Pileser I</a> names the lands of Daiaeni among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nairi">Nairi</a> peoples he fought around 1112 BCE and captured its king Sien, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> defeated its king Asia in 845 BCE. The union seems to have centred on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and Chorokhi valleys.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its rulers commanded fortified settlements and a mixed economy of herding, farming and metalworking in rich ore country. Urartian annals record large tributes of gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, which suggests a wealthy, organised society, though nothing survives in its own words. Many scholars regard its people as early Kartvelian speakers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Diauehi was worn down by repeated campaigns of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menua">Menua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argishti-i">Argishti I</a>, and its king Utupurshi was forced into tribute. By about 760 BCE it was absorbed, and its northern lands passed into the orbit of the rising <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/diauehi.jpg" alt="Diauehi" width="320" height="229"><figcaption>Image: ArnoldPlaton · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diauehi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1120 BCE – 760 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sien, Asia, Utupurshi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Diaokhi, Daiaeni, Tao</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-940&amp;focus=diauehi" aria-label="Diauehi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5522 766 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Diauehi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0M6077 1051h0M6019 930h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5842 1022h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5842 1022h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Turkey. <a href="/atlas?year=-940&amp;focus=diauehi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
